#################################################################
#    Outcome relationship validation
#################################################################

:OutcomeRelationshipShape a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass :OutcomeRelationship ;
  sh:targetSubjectsOf :useCase , :targetOutcome , :contributionDescription ;
  sh:targetObjectsOf use-case:outcomeLink ;
  sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
  sh:pattern "^urn:uuid:[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}$" ;
  sh:not [ sh:class :Outcome ] ;
  sh:not [ sh:class use-case:UseCase ] ;
  sh:property [
    sh:path [ sh:alternativePath (
      :useCase [ sh:inversePath use-case:outcomeLink ]
    ) ] ;
    sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ; sh:class use-case:UseCase ;
    sh:message "An outcome relationship must identify exactly one UseCase IRI, counting useCase and inverse outcomeLink assertions together."@en
  ] ;
  sh:property [
    sh:path :targetOutcome ;
    sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ; sh:class :Outcome ;
    sh:message "An outcome relationship must identify exactly one target Outcome IRI."@en
  ] ;
  sh:property [
    sh:path :contributionDescription ;
    sh:or ( [ sh:datatype xsd:string ] [ sh:datatype rdf:langString ] ) ;
    sh:pattern "\\S" ;
    sh:message "A supplied contribution description must be nonblank plain or language-tagged text."@en
  ] ;
  rdfs:label "Outcome Relationship Shape"@en ;
  rdfs:comment "Validates UUID URN relationship identities, separation from endpoint roles, one use case counted in either direction, one outcome, and optional nonblank contribution text. Predicate targets also validate incomplete untyped relationship descriptions."@en ;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://ekgf.org/ontology/outcome#> .

:ValidationShapeSet a owl:NamedIndividual , ekg-shape:ShapeSet ;
  ekg-shape:hasShapesGraph <https://ekgf.org/ontology/outcome#> ;
  ekg-shape:hasShape :OutcomeRelationshipShape ;
  ekg-shape:shapePurpose ekg-shape:Validation ;
  rdfs:label "Outcome Relationship Validation Shapes"@en ;
  rdfs:comment "The Outcome Ontology's relationship validators, including their nested endpoint and contribution-text constraints."@en ;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://ekgf.org/ontology/outcome#> .

#################################################################
#    Markdown explanations
#################################################################

<https://ekgf.org/ontology/outcome#> annotation:markdownDescription """
## Outcomes and contributions

An Outcome records a shared business result. An OutcomeRelationship records
one use case's contribution to that result, with its own UUID URN identity,
one `useCase`, one `targetOutcome`, and optional `contributionDescription`.
The inverse `use-case:outcomeLink` supports navigation from the use case.
Several use cases can contribute to the same outcome with separate descriptions.

The [Method's Outcome Relationship](https://method.ekgf.org/concept/outcome/outcome-relationship/#ontology)
provides the source model. OWL declares endpoint types, exact cardinalities,
and separation of relationship and endpoint roles. SHACL checks the supplied
resource terms, identifiers and text; validate before OWL identity reasoning.

`use-case:hasOutcome` is an unqualified summary that OWL can derive from
`use-case:outcomeLink` followed by `targetOutcome`. The summary does not identify
a relationship, record its contribution, or establish ownership. Direct summary
assertions do not manufacture relationship objects. `hasOutcome` in this
namespace and `story:contributesTo` retain their direct-outcome meanings.

Relationships referencing an ancestor's outcome must be stated explicitly.
Use-case parentage and reuse create no automatic outcome links, contributions
or ownership assignments. Outcome priority concerns the outcome itself. The
Method does not define confidence or contribution-priority scales; these
properties and an explicit outcome-ownership model remain separate decisions.

All entry and nested validators belong to `ValidationShapeSet`, which links to
this module's concrete shapes graph through `ekg-shape:hasShapesGraph`.
See the [relationship guide](https://github.com/EKGF/ekg-ontologies/blob/main/docs/outcome-relationships.md)
for a complete example, query, validation command and snapshot semantics.
"""@en .

:OutcomeRelationship annotation:markdownDescription """
## One identified contribution

Each relationship connects exactly one UseCase to exactly one Outcome. Use a
stable UUID URN such as `urn:uuid:fa48a552-6f45-4f01-872e-0146622295d6` for its
identity; generate a fresh random UUIDv4 when creating a relationship and keep
it when labels or descriptions change. The validation profile checks the
hyphenated UUID URN syntax. It does not prove random generation or global
uniqueness, and does not constrain the UUID version.

The relationship is disjoint from UseCase and Outcome. It is not the outcome
itself. Its endpoints are functional, but neither endpoint identifies the
relationship: sharing a source, a target, or both never merges relationship
resources. Multiple relationships for the same pair are permitted and can
carry separate contribution descriptions. No pair-based identity key is set.

The relationship describes contribution. It does not make the source use case
the owner of the outcome. Parent and child use cases can explicitly refer to
the same shared outcome; this does not copy that outcome or automatically
create relationships for other descendants.
"""@en .

:useCase annotation:markdownDescription """
## Source use case

Identifies the single use case whose contribution this OutcomeRelationship
describes. `use-case:outcomeLink` is its inverse, declared in the Use Case
Ontology. Load both ontology modules for their complete OWL relationships.
A use case can participate in several relationship objects.

The validator counts `useCase` objects and inverse `use-case:outcomeLink`
subjects together without requiring inverse inference. Repeating the same
source in both directions counts once; two different source terms fail.
Functional OWL properties can equate endpoint names, so validate the supplied
terms before applying OWL identity reasoning.
"""@en .

:targetOutcome annotation:markdownDescription """
## Shared outcome, separate contributions

Identifies exactly one Outcome for this relationship. Many relationships can
target that same Outcome, including contributions from different use cases.
The outcome's definition, classification, lifecycle and outcome-level priority
remain on the shared Outcome; the contribution description stays on each
OutcomeRelationship.

This property is functional, not inverse-functional. It does not limit an
outcome to one contributing use case or equate relationships sharing a target.
The validator requires one supplied Outcome IRI and rejects multiple target
terms before OWL functionality could equate those names.
"""@en .

:contributionDescription annotation:markdownDescription """
## How this use case contributes

Optional text describing the source use case's contribution to the target
outcome. Use plain strings or language-tagged literals. Multiple translations
are supported. Every supplied value must contain a non-whitespace character;
numbers, resource IRIs, empty strings and whitespace-only text fail validation.

This description belongs to the relationship. Different use cases can share
one outcome while explaining different contributions, and an absent description
does not invalidate an otherwise complete relationship. The text does not
assert that an outcome has been achieved, assign ownership or execute metrics.
"""@en .

:OutcomeRelationshipShape annotation:markdownDescription """
## Validate supplied contribution records

Targets OutcomeRelationship instances and subclasses, subjects of `useCase`,
`targetOutcome` and `contributionDescription`, and objects of
`use-case:outcomeLink`. Predicate targets prevent incomplete records from
escaping validation merely by omitting an explicit relationship type.

Requires a UUID URN identifier, exactly one source UseCase IRI counted in both
directions, exactly one target Outcome IRI, and nonblank text for every supplied
description. Relationship resources cannot also be UseCases or Outcomes.
With no inference, endpoint classes must be supplied; RDFS inference with
both ontology modules can derive endpoint types from property domains/ranges.

Validate one coherent version context before OWL identity reasoning. Different
endpoint-version IRIs count as different supplied terms in a merged graph.
This shape validates relationship instances; it does not require every UseCase
to have one, determine business ownership, infer inheritance or assess whether
the contribution description is accurate.
"""@en .

:ValidationShapeSet annotation:markdownDescription """
## Outcome relationship validators

Registers OutcomeRelationshipShape and the nested shapes reachable from it.
The set has the Validation purpose and links to the Outcome Ontology module's
ShapesGraph IRI through `ekg-shape:hasShapesGraph`. The set and graph have
separate identities.

Load the module as the shapes graph for relationship validation. Load endpoint
types in the data, or supply the Outcome and Use Case ontology graphs with
RDFS inference. Set membership and the graph link do not fetch RDF sources,
select a historical snapshot, run validation or assert conformance by themselves.
"""@en .
